nd into the 60s that changes what  what drove the change and then what  happened the major changes the increase  in the prison population so for this  convict code to work effectively other  inmates have to know who you are and you  have to know who other inmates are but  as prisons get larger and larger the  prison population goes from fewer than  5,000 inmates up to 25,000 and  subsequently you know substantially more  there's more strangers around you and  that means that that ability for a  decentralized system to work works much  and and of course everybody is wearing  the same clothes so they you know you  can't you can't even really distinguish  people by you know most physical  appearances  that gives rise partly both to gangs who  then organize and racial gangs right  yeah absolutely so in the 1960s in  California there's dramatic increases in  inmate on inmate violence many more  stabbings and assaults and that reflects  the fact that the code is less effective  at providing the safety in order than  inmates one so what we see when we study  the history of gangs so prison gangs  like the Mexican Mafia the Aryan  Brotherhood and these notorious groups  when you look at their histories they  all form to provide protection and  security and safety to their own members  so in the 1960s and 70s they're forming  because as an alternative source of  governance to this convict code and as  populations increase increase  subsequently these gangs take on île  even more important and and one of the  reasons the race is important is because  again when you're everybody's dressed  the same that's one signifier where you  can guess ok if you're if you're white  and you see now the right guy they're on  the same team if you're black and black  you're in the same team Mexico what the  gang politics as they call it works is  it's a it's effective at doing what  inmates want because you don't have to  know a particular person you just have  to know which group they affiliate with  so that without a doubt there's there's  racist prison gang members I don't want  to deny that yeah but there's a logic to  it in that in an environment where Evan  wears the same clothes they have similar  haircuts they're in a roughly similar  age what's the easiest way to identify  what groups someone affiliates with well  the color of your skin is very low-cost  to identify and that makes that that  interaction between members of different  groups much easier to go it happens if  you you and you see this and shows like  Oz and whatnot or you know prison shows  if you're say you're white and the the  Aryan Nation gang comes along or  something and say hey you got to join us  what happens to the people who choose  not to yeah well I'm in California the  affiliation that you have to make isn't  typically with kind of the one of the  traditional prison gangs it's just with  a broader racial group the white inmates  may be from a certain geographic area as  long as you're willing to affiliate to  that limited extent then that's good  enough if you're not willing to do that  sometimes inmates affiliate with  religious groups or sports teams but  what's key to each of those groups is  that each member of the group is  responsible for every other  so when there's problems everyone knows  who to go to to try to control you talk  a little bit about tattoos as well  because this is you know it's a  flourishing industry or practice in  prison with rudimentary tools but why  how do tattoos function and one of the  prison is an environment where there's  lots of strangers around you you don't  know who's trustworthy who's dangerous  who has a good reputation and who has a  bad one tattoos are a way to provide  credible information they're permanent  they're often prominently placed so  you're not just going to get a tattoo on  your face your forehead indicating that  you have some past experience or  affiliation if you haven't because if  you did you couldn't very easily hide it  so it makes some great very credible  signals of information and in a world of  so many untrustworthy people and  strangers they're especially important  and this is they show up you know at the  at the hands above the neck etc right  because there's the whole idea I mean it  might be you know if we're we get drunk  and we go to a tattoo parlor we get one  on our butt that's the last place you  want to tattoo in prison right you want  it to be visible the more visible it is  the more information it provides to  strangers and the more the more easy it  is for other people to know what that  information is you're trying what was  driving the increase in the prison  population in the 50s and 60s well I  mean a variety of different factors the  big increase in the prison population  comes later in the 1970s and importantly  the 1980s and 90s and that's because  we're incarcerated people for longer  we're ramping up the drug war of fish  prosecutors are prosecuting more of the  cases that they're that erupted to them  so a variety of different factors all  contribute to this this big boom and as  you know I mean the United States locks  up more people than any country in the  world and that's driving or creating the  foundations for the importance of prison  gangs today yeah and and you say to  minimize prison gangs or the need for  prison gangs you you've got to minimize  the prison population prison gangs are  not ideal nobody wants these to be the  main source of order in prison they  don't practice accountability quality  rule of law anything like that so if we  want them to be less important we need  to make prisons smaller and safer so  that inmates on that return to gangs for  protection are you optimistic about  prison reform efforts going on now  particularly sentencing reform  some people like Rand Paul and a bunch  of other politicians really moving  forward it seems on sentencing it seems  that there is an interest and an  emphasis from from both